<h2>What is overhyped (or just not ready yet)</h2>

<p>Not everything labelled "AI" is worth your attention. Here are the areas where the marketing has gotten ahead of the reality.</p>

<p><strong>"Fully autonomous" sales agents.</strong> The idea that an AI can run your entire sales process end to end, from first contact to closed deal, sounds incredible. In practice, it is not there yet for most small businesses. Customers can tell when they are talking to a bot that is pretending to be human, and trust erodes quickly. AI is excellent as a first responder and a support tool. It is not a replacement for the relationship-building that closes deals in service businesses.</p>

<p><strong>AI-generated websites.</strong> You can generate an entire website with AI in minutes. The problem is that it looks like an AI-generated website. Generic layouts, stock imagery, placeholder copy that sounds like every other business in your category. Your website is your first impression. It deserves more thought than "generate and publish".</p>

<p><strong>Predictive analytics for small data sets.</strong> Enterprise AI can predict customer churn, forecast revenue, and identify trends because it has millions of data points to work with. A small business with 200 customers and 18 months of data does not have enough for meaningful predictions. The tool might give you numbers, but those numbers are not reliable enough to base decisions on. Trust your instincts and your direct customer conversations instead.</p>

<p><strong>"AI-powered" everything.</strong> Some tools slap "AI" on features that are really just basic automation or rule-based logic. Sending a <a href="/blog/automate-follow-up">follow-up email when someone fills out a form</a> is not AI. That is a simple trigger. Be wary of tools that use AI as a marketing term without explaining what the AI actually does differently.</p>

<p><strong>Voice AI for inbound calls.</strong> The technology is improving fast, but today, most voice AI still sounds robotic, struggles with accents, and cannot handle complex or emotional conversations. For a plumber or a physiotherapist, having a robot answer your phone can feel impersonal. A missed-call text-back automation is simpler, cheaper, and often more effective.</p>

<h2>How to evaluate AI features without getting distracted</h2>

<p>When a tool tells you it has AI features, ask these three questions:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>What task does this replace or speed up?</strong> If the answer is vague ("it makes everything smarter"), move on. If the answer is specific ("it drafts email replies based on your conversation history"), that is worth testing.</li>
  <li><strong>Does it require me to learn a new workflow?</strong> The best AI features fit into what you are already doing. If you need a tutorial, a prompt engineering course, or a certification to use it, it is probably built for a different audience.</li>
  <li><strong>Can I turn it off without losing anything?</strong> Good AI features are additive. They make things better when they work and do not break anything when they do not. If the entire system depends on AI working perfectly every time, that is a fragile setup.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p>AI should feel like a helpful assistant, not a new full-time job to manage.</p></blockquote>

